I apologize to anyone who took offense to my response to Otis Elevator,
regarding DeskStation systems.

I thought that there were a few EMail  conventions that were fairly well
 understood and didn't need explaining.  I hate to waste reader's bandwidth
 but it seems necessary here.

Terms:
BTW - translates to "by the way"
IMHO - translates to "in my humble opinion"

If anyone cares, I'd invite them to re-read my response to Otis Elevator in
Australia with those items in mind.  It may change the way my response is
interpreted.  I don't want this to sound like sour grapes, but I think this 
Carrera1 response was uncalled for.  I'd bet that this person is 
NOT a Digital employee.

With that much said, I'll add.
Yes, DEC has been showing EV-56 based systems. [Anyone else?]
DeskStation IS SHIPPING systems based on the EV56 chips.  400MHz and
433MHz systems are available as of a few weeks ago, the 333MHz and
366MHz systems have been shipping since weeks before NAB [mid April].
There are a few clocked-up to 333MHz EV-5 systems out there.  I don't
 think that any other manufacturer, clone or otherwise, can make the EV-56 
claim.  I may be wrong, and I'm sure someone will tell me if I am.

Regarding the relative merit of the DEC and DeskStation designs.  There
are known, conscious trade-offs with putting the processor, L3 cache, 
support chipset and BIOS on a card [the DeskStation design].  There IS 
a full 256 bit wide path to (faster) L3 cache but a 128 bit wide path to the (not
so fast) system RAM.  What does this design on a card get you??  The
ability to swap everything associated with a processor - upgradability 
- across processor generations or, more broadly, processor independence.  
[The EV-56 is basically a lower voltage, die-shrink of the EV-5]

I'd also like to refer you to ClieNT Server News as a good source for
 information.  (516) 759-7025.  I don't always believe everything I read,
thus the "IMHO" preface to some of my statements.  The reports of the
Alcor chipset being buggy come from the pages of that mag, as well as,
the demise of the Mizar chipset which was supposedly slated for the 
EV-56, etc.  And the scramble back to Alcor [EV-5] to adapt it to the lower
voltage EV-56.

Additionally,  the new from DEC, "Brett" reference motherboard design
appears to be targeted at the low end PC market.  Three PCI and 2 ISA
slots don't make for much in the way of expansion capability.  Decent 
video, SCSI controller, and ethernet card leave you with a functional 
system, but that's about it. With market trends heading toward 
MORE PCI slots, I'm not so sure of impact of systems based on this
design.  Since these motherboards should be available from Wyle, 
this may be a blow to those clone manufacturers that rely heavily on
reference designs from DEC.......  

Best,
cjm
