David,
 
  To use your local Compuserve dialup point as a generic Internet network
port, install the Remote Access Service in your Win NT system.  It's
probably already installed.

  I forget what options come up when installing RAS for the first time, but
if asked, make sure RAS is configured to support TCP/IP protocol.  If RAS is
already installed, go to Control Panel, Networks, select Remote Access
Service, and click the Configure button.  In the dialog that pops up listing
your modem(s), click the Network button.  Make sure TCP/IP is selected in
the network protocol dialog that pops up.  Netbeui and IPX are not used for
Internet stuff.  Close all the dialogs.

  Edit the text file \winnt\system32\ras\switch.inf in a text editor like
Notepad.
At the end of the file add a section like this:

[CompuServe]
; get to the host name prompt
COMMAND=<cr>
OK=<match>":"
; log on to the CIS network
COMMAND=CIS<cr>
; check for user name prompt
OK=<match>":"
; enter the user name
COMMAND=<username>/GO:PPPCONNECT<cr>
; check for the password prompt
OK=<match>":"
; enter the password
COMMAND=<password><cr>
; finish
OK=<ignore>

DON'T put your actual password or username in this script.  <username> and
<password> are macros which RAS will replace with the username and password
stored in your personal RAS phonebook when you log in to connect to that
network name.  This is particularly important on a machine used by multiple
people.

Save the text file and close notepad.

Run the RAS dialer, RASPHONE.EXE.  It should be in your system32 directory.
Click Add to define a new phone book entry.
Enter a name for the phone book entry, and the local Compuserve phone
number.  Include any special dialing prefixes or suffixes in this number.  

Deselect "Authenticate using current username and password", unless your NT
user name is the same as your Compuserve id.  <g>

If the third button on the right is "Advanced>>>", click it.  This will
expand the dialog to show a row of buttons along the bottom edge.  I don't
know why they hide these, since it's impossible to configure a RAS phonebook
entry without them...

Click on the Network button to bring up the "Network Protocol Settings" dialog.
Select PPP, and within it, select TCP/IP and "Request LCP extensions".  You
don't need Netbeui or IPX.

Click the TCP/IP settings button to bring up the "PPP TCP/IP settings" dialog.
Select "Server Assigned IP Address".  Select both the checkboxes at the
bottom of the dialog.

In the middle of the "PPP TCP/IP Settings" dialog is an option to specify a
DNS name server IP address, or to let RAS obtain a DNS server address at
connect time.   Server-assigned DNS from Compuserve never worked for me, so
I use the specific addresses (149.174.64.41) primary and (149.174.64.42)
backup.  These servers are almost certainly located in the US (in Ohio), so
you should eventually ask Compuserve for a DNS server that's more local to
your hemisphere.  These should work, but talking to them from Australia will
take longer than a local DNS server, and you use DNS a lot when surfing the Web.

Close the rat's nest of dialogs until you get back to the "Edit Phone Entry"
dialog.  Click the security button.

In the security settings dialog, select "Accept any authentication...", then
click on the "After Dialing" combo box at the bottom and select "Compuserve"
from the list.  This list comes from switch.inf.

That should be just about everything.  Close all the dialogs til you reach
the RASPHONE main window, then double click on the compuserve entry.  You'll
be prompted for a user name and password.  Enter your Compuserve user ID for
the user name (using a period between the numbers instead of a comma) and
your Compuserve password for the password.  RAS will remember the user name,
but you'll have to enter the password each time you connect.  That, or enter
your actual password in the plain text Switch.inf that anyone on your
machine can see.

At the bottom of the Authentication dialog, enter COMPUSERVE.COM as your
domain name.  Click Ok, and watch the fun begin!  RAS should initialize your
modem, dial the phone number, sync with the other modem, log you into
Compuserve, and then minimize itself.  You should now be able to run normal
Winsock applications such as Netscape over the RAS link.  Do NOT try to use
WinCIM's web browser over a network link - WinCIM is hardcoded for its own
modem connection layer.

Oh, if you have an internal modem, you'll want to enable the Launch Monitor
at Startup option in the Options menu of RASPHONE, so you can see the
transmit/receive activity on the link.  Helps make the slow modem speed
bearable - at least you know something's going on when the browser pauses
for a while waiting for material from the net.  You can also hide the
monitor window's menu and caption to shrink it down really small.

Incidentally, these steps will make RAS work with just about any Internet
service provider that supports PPP.  The only variable really is the
Switch.inf script to enter the login info into the dialup host.  The default
"Generic Login" script handles most Unix-type logins.

Hope this helps,

-Danny Thorpe
 dthorpe@subliminal.com

