>
>Criss Laidlaw (server%20&%20server->printer%20protocols">claidlaw@gmpvt.com)

Hi Criss,

Sure, you can do that.  The NT system can access the printer via DLC,   
then you can use LPR to get to the NT queue from the VMS machine.  Once   
the job is on the server, it no longer cares what protocol was used to   
get it there, it just knows that it needs to get it to the printer and   
will use whatever protocol is required to do that.

However, LPR will create a queue on the VMS host -- there's no way around   
that.  So, you're really going to have 2 queues regardless.  The bene of   
putting LPD on the printer itself is that then you would have one   
protocol between the printer and both queues, but you'd also have   
redundant paths to the printer (you know exactly what your VMS users are   
going to say when the NT server goes down, for any reason, and they can't   
get to their printer!)  (hmm, and vice-versa).  The biggest drawback here   
is that it may take 2 minutes (the default retry interval, if I remember   
correctly) to get a printout to come out if the NT system is sending a   
job.  Of course, that setting can be configured down, too.

JMHO (that's "Just My Humble Opinion" ;^)

Aaron
