From digital1@enet.net Sat Jun  8 12:40:56 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0488.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <199606080545.AAA16257@foo.garply.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Jun 1996 10:28:25 -0700
From: Charles J. McCallion <digital1@enet.net>
Subject: FW: Very small LAN (A How To Question)

First, my condolences on having to run Win95! -- this OS is 
PROOF that software can be demonically possessed.

Also, I don't think with your configuration that your two Intel 
systems will see each other.  You basically have your Server
set up as a router which NetBEUI may not cope with.  You may
better luck with NWLink / IPX / SPX.

One of the first rules when you troubleshoot is to get to the
minimum configuration that you can stand and start from there.
In your case, I can't tell if this setup was working prior to getting
the P5/166.  SO, try using just one Intel system, cross-over cable
and just one NIC in the Server.  Make sure you have good bindings
 one both sides.  
On the Server system, get to Control Panel / Networks.  Remove
ALL Network components including the adapters'drivers.  Shutdown.
Open the system, take out the 2nd NIC.  Restart.  Reinstall the 
driver for the NIC that's still in the system,  as well as NetBEUI.  
There will be a bunch of other components that will be installed, too.
You DID set up server as a Primary Domain Controller when you 
installed, right??  Setting server up as just a server won't provide
the kind of authentication you expect.  If you set up as "just" server
you can have what's called "implicit trust", where when you attempt
to connect across the network, NT recognizes your Name / Password
as the same as one of its local accounts and gives you access.  In this
case YOU must keep the Win95 systems' accounts and the accounts
on the server synchronized. 

On the Win95 box, get into Control Panel.  Go to
System and select the Device Manager tab.  Make sure there are
no device conflicts.  Next go to Networks in Control Panel.  You'll
want to have the following:  Client for MS networks, your NIC,
NetBEUI -> "your NIC", and File & Print sharing [at least] in the
upper part of the dialog box.  In the drop-down you'll need to select
Client for MS Networks - NOT Windows Logon.  On the identification
tab: These three entries are pretty self explanatory.  Under the Access
Control tab, pick Use Level access and put in the name of your Domain
Controller, [this presumes that there is one on the net, see above].  
Restart this system.

Basically, work getting two systems to talk first.  When successful, then 
use the same technique to get the second system working using the
same cable and NIC in the server, that is, take the 486 off the net, get
the P5/166 talking.  THEN, install the second NIC in the server and
proceed from there.

Hope this helps some,

cjm

