From: Nebojsa Novakovic <nova@pacific.net.sg>
Subject: DeskStation Raptor Reflex 400 MHz

Hi all Alpha NT fans,

This hot discussion between the supporters of Alcor designs (DEC and all
cloners, including Aspen etc) and DeskStation Raptor is a good proof to me
that Alpha architecture is really getting popular.

Let's have some things clear:
The majority of today's Alpha cloners use DEC Semiconductor EB164 21164 EV5
full-AT sized reference board, originally meant to assist the system
developers, in their machines. Aspen's Baby-AT 'Summit' is the only
exception, but even it has only one major difference to EB164 - smaller
board format, otherwise it is almost identical in basic features. So most
cloners actually seem to be just system integrators using DEC boards in the
same way some big PC vendors (Gateway 2000, Dell, Packard Bell) might be
assembling Intel stuff. Please correct me if I'm wrong.

DeskStation's big plus is that Raptors are completely their own designs,
down to the chipset and HAL for Windows NT. Yes, the memory bus on
first-generation Raptors is slower than on Alcors (128-bit page-mode DRAM
compared to 128-bit 2-way interleaved page-mode DRAM - DEC calls it
256-bit), and the latency is also slightly longer. However, these guys were
regularly able to beat DEC and any other Alpha vendor six months or more in
offering the newest Alpha chip in their systems - a proof is their 433 MHz
EV56 offering at the time others still can't deliver you any EV56 system now
(Boards with 21172 Alcor Bright for 21164A are still not out). And, as with
any chipset and system design, you can expect Deskstation to improve their
cache and memory bandwidth in their forthcoming offerings - look at their
web page about the new Multiflex quad-Alpha. Also, as more money comes into
the game, their EPLD-based system logic could be integrated into cheaper and
more compact ASICs.  A good point of EPLD is still that any bugs can be
cleaned in a matter of minutes or at most hours via simple EPLD
reprogramming, compared to Digital hanging at LSI Logic (the actual
manufacturer of Alcor chipsets) for weeks to change buggy ASICs: who is late
at the end - look at past one year!

All this doesn't mean DeskStation is a perfect vendor, but at least they
took the risk to do some actual R&D and come out with unique, successful
systems well known even here in Singapore. They deserve thumbs up for that
effort, considering how small the company is.

Nebojsa Novakovic
