You may right in the reference to Deskstation and their Alpha systems R&D.
About three months ago I contacted them to get a Raptor.  There was hitch
however.  We are slow---ly moving from VMS to NT.  And given the strengths
of VMS in many areas, we may be many years with it.  I needed a box that
could run VMS or NT.  Run our apps under VMS now, move to NT in the future.
Raptors cannot run VMS.  Except for Digital, when I requested quotes,
there was only one vendor who could supply VMS on 21164, that was MicroWay.
Aspen could on a 21064, but not on a 21164.  That may be different today,
but not when I needed it.  Deskstation systems to my understanding will never
run VMS.  So the argument for EB164 can be made that it can handle VMS as 
well as NT.

I have the MicroWay Screamer, and it is indeed much faster than my Alpha 2100
server, and much cheaper than an Alphastation 500 or 600 from DEC.  So if
anyone needs a hot VMS box (or DEC Unix) that can run NT in the future like I
did, contact Mike Browne at MicroWay (508) 746-7341.

I should say, I like the design of the Raptors, but I have many Pixar's here
with 192 bit buses (simultaneous read/write ie 192 bits in, 192 bits out)
and they can still do things faster than most 64 bit systems with any 
processor.  So I like the 128 bit 2-way interleaved design over just 128 bits.

