Date: Mon, 10 Jun 1996 10:37:44 -0500 (EST)
From: SINCLAIR@orph01.phy.ornl.gov <SINCLAIR@orph01.phy.ornl.gov>
Subject: NT Backup

vaughan@utxvms.cc.utexas.edu (Curt Vaughan) asks if and NT partition
can be backed up while running VMS.

The obvious issue here is backing up files currently opened by the
system - a problem for all sophisticated operating systems.

VMS has a very nice utility called "standalone backup" in which you
bring up a tiny memory resident environment that supports all devices
and file systems but which will only run the backup program. This
environment opens no system files and thus they may be backed-up
with no problem.

I was under the impression, perhaps mistakenly, that NT solved this
problem with the regback/regrest command. So, the following procedure
should give a complete backup of the system:

  1) stop all applications and disable shares
  2) run regback
  3) perform backup

Resoring a backup is given by

  1) perform backup ( in restore mode )
  2) run regrest
  3) reboot

Unfortunately, I don't write from experience. I would appreciate
hearing from others who could confirm or correct this procedure.

For additional comment: I installed NT Workstation 3.5 (my home
version) on a SCSI zip cartidge. This gives a complete standalone
environment for backing up the hard disk. In addition, one may create
a bootable floppy by formatting a floppy undet NT and copying the
appropriate files (I cannot remember which at the moment but will
gather the info for any interested parties). In this way, a hard disk
that fails to boot but is in every other way undamaged may be examined
and corrected by booting from the floppy/zip cartidge combination.

