On 10 Jun 96 at 11:29, Van Valkenburg, Brian (ON49) wrote:
> [snip]
> How can I check to see who the owner of the scheduler is (I cant find it in 
> the registry).

The scheduler runs as SYSTEM. However this has not be so. In our setup we 
have the scheduler service running as a defined user that is a member of 
the BACKUP OPERATORS group. This way it has permissions to access 
directories. To change the user that owns this service go into control 
panel, services and select the service you want (scheduler in this case). 
Then select startup, click on the "This Account" and fill in the user 
name and password. Beware that if you type a wrong password the system 
does not complain but then the service fails to start (you'll only catch 
this in the event log). You will have to stop and restart the service after 
you made the changes so that they come into effect.

I hope this helps
Pedro.

