Subject: RE: Command Scheduler Owner

owner-alphant wrote:
>
>Good day,
>
>I am having problems with a backup that is being performed by the   
command
>scheduler. Backups fail on remote NT workstations disks with an error   
saying
>that I don't have permission to access the disk.
>
>However.
>
>The same backup batch file works fine when I run it. (not using the
>scheduler).
>
>How can I check to see who the owner of the scheduler is (I cant find it   
in
>the registry).
>
>
>Thanks in Advance
>
>Brian V

Several people have recommended setting the AT service to run as a   
separate account in the backup admins group.  I personally would prefer   
*NOT* to do that.  This opens a security hole in that anyone who can   
schedule a job on the system now has access to *all* the files on the   
system.  Not good.

What I do instead is to provide a common account on all the machines in   
the backup group (call it BACKUP) with a common password (say PWORD).   
 Now, I can create a procedure on my machine with the tape device that   
does the following:

 net use X: \\node\c$ /user:node\BACKUP PWORD

You've got to provide both the username and password on this NET USE   
line, otherwise the connection will be made as the system (or would it be   
guest access?  I've not tried it, so I couldn't say.)  Either way, now   
I've got access to all the files on that drive and can back it up.  No   
changing the AT service, either.

Ah, but those of you enlightened in security matters say "WHOA!  You   
stuck a password in a file!!!"  Yup, sure did.  Sorry 'bout that.  This   
obviously needs to be an extremely tightly controlled file, or you've got   
another security breach.  Any other ideas on how else to provide password   
protected access?  You definitely don't want to provide the password as a   
parameter to the procedure passed from the AT command (just do an AT and   
you'd see it).  Can it be encrypted somehow?  Not that I'm aware of.  A   
proxy?  That's basically what you're doing in a workgroup, which requires   
the password.

Below is my procedure to actually do the backups.  Note that this is a   
modular routine and is called from another procedure with varying   
parameters for different workstations and drives.  Schedule this other   
procedure, not this one (don't forget to change the PASSWORD  on line   
15).

 ------------------------ cut here ---------------------------
rem @echo off
rem System_backup.bat
rem  by ACSakovich, 12/18/95
rem
rem Parameters: 1 2 3 4+
rem   Domain Node Disk Other optional parameters to NTbackup
rem   Name Name Share (up to 6)
rem  e.g.   WGRP NODE C$ /d "Joe's node" /a /t normal
echo ------------------------------------------------
time < nul: | findstr "current"
echo Backing up \\%2\%3 in domain %1
echo.
if %computername% == %2% goto Local
echo *** Connecting to remote machine
set drive=X
if exist %drive%:\. net use %drive%: /d
net use %drive%: \\%2\%3 /user:%1\Backup !!!YourPasswordHere!!!
if errorlevel 1 goto NoConn
echo *** Backing up remote drive
goto Backup

:Local
set drive=%3
echo *** Backing up local drive

:Backup
ntbackup backup %drive%: /v /b /l "d:\users\backup\logfiles\%1%2%3.log"   
/e %4 %5 %6 %7 %8 %9

if %computername% == %2% goto Done
echo *** Disconnecting remote machine
net use %drive%: /d

:Done
echo *** Exiting
:NoConn
 ------------------------ cut here ---------------------------

Hope this helps,
Aaron
