>You may right in the reference to Deskstation and their Alpha systems R&D.
>About three months ago I contacted them to get a Raptor.  There was hitch
>however.  We are slow---ly moving from VMS to NT.  And given the strengths
>of VMS in many areas, we may be many years with it.  I needed a box that
>could run VMS or NT.  Run our apps under VMS now, move to NT in the future.
>Raptors cannot run VMS.  Except for Digital, when I requested quotes,
>there was only one vendor who could supply VMS on 21164, that was MicroWay.
>Aspen could on a 21064, but not on a 21164.  That may be different today,
>but not when I needed it.  Deskstation systems to my understanding will never
>run VMS.  So the argument for EB164 can be made that it can handle VMS as 
>well as NT.
>
>I have the MicroWay Screamer, and it is indeed much faster than my Alpha 2100
>server, and much cheaper than an Alphastation 500 or 600 from DEC.  So if
>anyone needs a hot VMS box (or DEC Unix) that can run NT in the future like I
>did, contact Mike Browne at MicroWay (508) 746-7341.
>
>I should say, I like the design of the Raptors, but I have many Pixar's here
>with 192 bit buses (simultaneous read/write ie 192 bits in, 192 bits out)
>and they can still do things faster than most 64 bit systems with any 
>processor.  So I like the 128 bit 2-way interleaved design over just 128 bits.
>
>Gary Muswick
>Nuclear Radiology
>Unversity Hospitals of Cleveland
>muswick@uhrad.com
>

Even that 2-way interleaved design gives you only 532 MB/s peak memory
bandwidth, equal to a Pentium Pro-200 with Intel 440FX or VIA Apollo Pro
chipsets using a single 64-bit bank of 66 MHz 50 ns BurstEDO DRAM (did you
know that Intel's new desktop Pentium Pro chipset supports Micron's
BurstEDO, but they do not officially say that because they consider it
dead?). Also, high-speed cache is something EV5 is not famous for - only
EV56 and EV6-based are able to run really fast 100+ MHz external cache
buses. In any case, 1 GB/s is a reasonable bandwidth for next-generation
uniprocessor EV-56 and EV-6 desktops looking at CPU demands - why DEC 21172
chipset doesn't offer it is not my problem; and then someone will show
STREAMS benchmarks around where Sun Ultras beat Alphas solely because of
greater main memory bandwidth, again...

I agree VMS and DEC UNIX are technically superior OS to current NT 3.51
release; however, NT is rapidly improving and, as we could all hear, Alpha
will have the honour to run the first 64-bit Cairo NT, supposedly on
Comdex/Fall 97, as some sources say (though I rather believe the final one
will stretch a bit beyond Christmas - in any case Intel Merced won't be out
for at least 9 months after that date, so Alpha's exclusivity will last for
a while). All this means Deskstation's decision to bet solely on NT has
certain reason. Another is that you do not want to compete with Digital
across the board - if they also had competing UNIX and VMS desktops, they'd
have a real enemy in DEC's SBU.

