From: James Livingston <jwl@ntmail.zso.dec.com>
Subject: RE: NT 4.0 Radical Suggestion

Steve,

Speaking for myself only, I'd mention just a couple of things in
response to your suggestion.  First, Digital can't post anything to
which Microsoft own the rights on the Web, period.  That includes
Windows NT 4.0 Beta for the Alpha architecture.  How Microsoft choose to
distribute their Betas is up to them, but it's worth noting that they
just mailed out 200,000 copies of Windows NT 4.0 Beta 2, both
Workstation and Server, to their Windows NT mailing list.  The products
are time-bombed, but that's O.K.; as you observe, users will likely buy
the production version when it comes out.  The presence of the time-bomb
pretty well commits Microsoft to delivering the product before it goes
off, right?

The fact that you got a copy of Windows NT 4.0 Beta from your friend
puts that friend in jeopardy, as it does you; you for using software you
don't have a license to run, your friend from violating his or her
license agreement with Microsoft.  These days, those violations are
being taken seriously, particularly when the U.S. is preparing to hit
China with billions of dollars in trade sanctions for failing to stop
wholesale piracy of software within its borders.  I'd feel especially
uncomfortable if I were in government service, and violating software
license agreements.

All that said, I wouldn't be so pessimistic about the delivery date for
4.0; Microsoft clearly have a big stake in getting it out as quickly as
is consistent with delivering a quality product.  It's going to generate
a significant amount of revenue for them, and starting that stream as
soon as possible is clearly in their interest.

Cheers,
James

