 Greetings,
     
  My brain has recently overloaded and I can't seem to
 see clearly on this one.
     
  I'm implementing a third-party document management system,
 namely Saros Document Server (Alpha 5/600 266, NT 3.51, SP3) with 
 Saros' Document Manager (NT Workstation 3.51, WfW3.11, W95) client 
 on the desktops. One of functions of this software is the ability 
 to drag-and-drop a selected document onto a "Print" drop-zone, 
 which in turn displays a print window for selecting queues, print 
 attributes, et al. The queues are either LPS20s (setup through
 the Digital Network Port) or Kodak 1392/Model 44s (setup through 
 lpr to queues on a Sun station).
     
  The printing of most documents (MS Word, Excel, HTML,
 text, etc) is fine. The problem is that the majority (and most 
 critcal) files to be printed are PostScript files. I have yet 
 been able to successfully print a PostScript file to either type 
 of printer. The resulting output is the actual PostScript code. 
 I've been told that the PSCRIPT.DRV is converting the PostScript 
 code to PostScript and printing that. I'm thinking that there may 
 be a dreaded control-D at position 1 of the file, causing the file 
 to be interpreted as an ASCII file. I somehow remember a registry 
 edit to strip the control-D, but remember my brain.
     
  Can anyone with experience in these matters clue me. The
 Saros software is, supposedly, highly customizable so that we 
 can replace the print window with another .DLL, if necessary.
     
 Any and all assistance is greatly appreciated...Angelo
