From netman@szem.szeg.matav.hu Mon Jun 24 08:41:36 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0702.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <2.2.16.19960624143830.523f4982@caemapc.hermia.fi>
Date: Mon, 24 Jun 1996 14:53:49 +0200
From: Mihaly HOMONNAI <netman@szem.szeg.matav.hu>
Subject: reinstalling Multia base SW from the scratch

Hi,

First of all, sorry for my poor English.
Maybe the folloving information will help someone who has a crashed Multia 
system.

My Multia crashed a few month ago. It wasn't abble to boot the NT. So i 
decided to reinstall the base SW.
I've a 3.0C Multia admin kit, and a CD drive connected to the Multia.
I can't manage how to recover the system directly from the CD (the manual 
doens't mention it), so i decided to install the CD on a host system.

I've a few VAX-es with VMS5.5-2 on them. So i installed the Admin kit on a 
VAX/VMS5.5-2/Multinet 3.3C machine. 
The Multia's administrator guide says about VMS 6.1 as a supported system, 
so I had trouble on installation. But i manage it.

So here is the step by step instruction installing the Multia system admin 
kit on a VAX/VMS5.5-2/Multinet 3.3C/Infoserver system, and then recovering 
the NT from the VAX host using BOOTP protocol.

Utilities you need to install the kit, and not included in the standard 
VMS5.5-2 kit:

- CD_MOUNT (i don't know where to get it, i think it is a share/freeware)
- FILE.EXE (a utility to change file attributes.
      You can find it at ftp.wku.edu:[.VMS.FILESERV]FILE.ZIP

A. MOUNTING THE CD ON THE INFOSERVER, AND ON THE VMS

1. insert the CD in the Infoserver CD driver.

2. connect to the Infoserver from  the VMS:

$ set host/lat infsrv
password: <type password>
InfoServer> show service

[now there'll appear the CD's disk label, and the device name]

InfoServer> create service MULTIA-CD for DK0: class ods_2

[it'll create a multia service. We'll use the 'multia' to bind the CD]
InfoServer>logout

3. Creating a device, and mounting it

$ mcr ess$ladcp bind /system MULTIA-CD
[ it'll create a device named _DAD<number>, like _DAD1:]

$ cd_mount /media=cdrom/system/undefined_fat=stream:512 -
    DAD1: DIGITAL_MULTIA_SYS_ADMIN_VOL1  multia-logical-name

[it'll mount the device dad1: wher is a cd with volume label: 
DIGITAL_MULTIA.... and will assign a logical name multia-logical-name.
I think, that the program 'cd_mount' is a a shareware utility. Not sure. ]

So now we have a device dad1: where we can reach the Multia admin kit.

$ dir dad1:[000000]
CDNOTE.TXT;1              1/9        6-SEP-1995 11:47:50.01
COPYRGHT.TXT;1            3/9        4-JAN-1995 19:24:28.01
KITDESC.DSC;1             1/9        1-SEP-1995 16:45:18.01
KITDESC.INI;1             2/9        1-SEP-1995 16:41:26.01
V30C.DIR;1                1/9       20-JUN-1996 14:11:01.71
 

B. INSTALLING THE KIT ON THE VMS HOST SYSTEM

4. Installing the kit

OK. Now this is a big time to run the @VMSINSTAL util to install the 
dad1:[v30c.install.openvms]multx030.* kit.

There is a little diverge between the CD, and the manual. The manual says 
(page 75) [install.vms] instead of [v30c.install.openvms] directory. And 
the manual says about the install kit name as MULTI030, isntead of 
MULTX030 that is realy in the [v30c.install.openvms] directory.

OK.

$ set def sys$update  ! changing default directory to the update dir
$ @vmsinstal

[it'll ask the mounted volume, and the product's name, and the destination 
device, where it'll create a MULTIA$LIBRARY directoty containing all the 
installed kit:
install dir: dad1:[v30c.install.openvms]
product: MULTX030 
dest dev: d12:   so there will be a d12:[multia$library] dir.

 And it'll fail now!!! :-(((

Because there are stream files in the directory, so the MULTX030.* aren't 
backup files. Now you can't continue the installation procedure, until you 
copy all the CD content to a disk drive,and change the file attributes.

5. Copy the whole CD to a hard disk.
 
I created the directory structure similar to the CD on a disk drive d12:, 
and copied the whole cd to these directories:

$ crea/dir d12:[multia]
$ crea/dir d12:[multia.v30c]
$ crea/dir d12:[multia.v30c.install]
...
$ set def dad1:[000000]  ! changing the default dir to the CD's root dir
$ copy/exclude=(*.dir) [...]*.* d12:[multia]/log

6. Change the file attributes in the 
d12:[multia.v30c.install.openvms]multx030.* to saveset:

$ file/rec=9216/max=9216/type=fixed d12:[multia.v30c.install.openvms]-
   multx030.*
(The openvms6.1 has the set file/attrib=(...) that does not present in 
5.5-2.)

7. I had a few more trouble observed later, so i tell the more file 
attributes to change now.

7.1. Extract the MULTX303.A BACKUP file to a dir fe.: d12:[tmp].:

$ backup multx030.a d12:[tmp]/save

7.2. Edit the d12:[tmp]kitinstall.com file:

Search for a string 'file/attrib' (there is only one line)
Comment out this line
Insert new lines after that:
$ fileattrib="$<where is the file.exe util>file.exe"
$! for example: fileattrib="$userdisk:[dir1.dir2]file.exe"
$ fileattrib/org=seq/type=fixed/rec=512/max=512 vmi$kwd:'filename'

7.3 Backup the files to the MULTX303.A file by:

$ del multx030.a;1
$ backup *.* d12:[multia.v30c.install.openvms]multx030.a

8. If you want to install the X server support too, change the attributes 
of image files in dir: [v30c.files.xsupport.openvms]:

$ file /type=fixed/rec=9216/max=9216 -
    d12:[v30c.files.xsupport.openvms]images.*
$ file /type=fixed/rec=9216/max=9216 -
    d12:[v30c.files.xsupport.openvms]vxt*.*

9. Run again the vmsinstall procedure:

$ set def sys$update
$ @vmsinstal
parameters are:
mounted device: d12:[multia.v30c.install.openvms]
product: MULTX030

then follow the instructions, choice the files you want to install, etc 
etc.

C. RECOVERING THE BASE SYSTEM ON THE MULTIA

I used the MULTINET's BOOTP and TFTP protocols to transfer files to the 
Multia from the VAX.
So i configured out the BOOTP and TFTP servers.

10. Configuring BOOTP on the VMS

10.1. Edit the multinet:bootp-server.configuration file.
 Add lines similar to these:

chaos:\
        :sm=255.255.255.0\
        :ht=ethernet:\
#       :vm=rfc1048:\
        :ha=08002be4741e:\
        :ip=148.246.13.2:\
        :bf="rmf.sys":

NOTE: I tried to add line :bf="rmf.sys": and :T144="rmf.sys":, but it 
seems doesn't work. I observer the bootp process using Multinet's tcpview 
utility, and the VAX didn't pass the 'boot file' parameter to the Multia 
during bootp.
 
Evrery time i changed the bootp config file, i restarted the server by:
$ multinet netcontrol bootp reload

10.2 after editing the file, restart the bootp server using:
$ multinet netcontrol bootp reload 

command. And of course prior enable the server, if it is disabled, and 
enable the IP address to use the bootp server at the multinet 
configire/menu section. I've already enabled.

11. CONFIGURING TFTP protocol on the VMS

11.1 Edit the multinet:tftp.filename-translations. It can be used to 
translate between tftp client filenames and VMS filenames.
Add lines similar to this:
rmf.sys                         D12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]RMF.SYS
/RMF.CMD                        d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]RMF.CMD
/ARC.SYS                        d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]ARC.SYS
/CDRIVE_M.TAZ                 d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]CDRIVE_M.TAZ
/DDRIVE_M.AA                    d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]DDRIVE_M.AA
/DDRIVE_M.AB                    d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]DDRIVE_M.AB
/DDRIVE_M.AC                    d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]DDRIVE_M.AC
/FIRM.CMD                       d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]FIRM.CMD
/SRM.SYS                        d12:[MULTIA$LIBRARY.V30C.MAINT]SRM.SYS

Where did i get these filenames? Hehe, i used the Multinet's tcpview 
utility to gather bootp and tftp requests from my Multia, and i found 
these filenames at tftp process.

NOTE: so there is the rmf.sys file pointer, but the Multia seems doesn't 
get it's name at bootp process. See later at the boot sequence.

11.2 Restart the tftp server by:
$ mul netc tftp reload

And now everything is ready to turn the Multia on. :-))

12. Turn the Multia on, and choice the OSF console as the manual 
describes.

13. Turn the computer off, and on. The >>> prompt appears indicating OSF 
console is ready.

14.The manual indicates, that you can boot by:

>>> boot -p bootp ew

I tried to boot this way, but as i mentioned below it hanged up, because 
the Multia did'nt get the proper boot filename.
So you can use the

>>>boot -file rmf.sys -p bootp ew

command to boot the machine.

And you can go away for a caffe now. The machine will boot, and download 
the whole base SW from the VAX. It will recover the machine, and destroy 
all the data on the HDD.

If the installation finish, you can issue command:

>>>arc

The arc console will appear, and you can boot the NT now.

I hope you'll find it hopefully.

Mihaly HOMONNAI

ps. a few question:
1. why the admin kit doesn't seem to be good for VMS5.5-2. Maybe the 6.1 
sees the cd correctly?

2. what would be a problem at the bootp configuration (i know that is a 
vms related question, but i've a few other devices booting from the 
multinet, and they work fine)?

