Subject: Re: FTP ACCESS ON THE FLY IN NT?

At 10:21 PM 6/24/1996 -0700, you wrote:
>Anyone know of any existing ftp server software (or script add-ons) I might
>be able to acquire that will allow me to program accesses that will take
>effect immediately, and that will both: 
>
>    a.  create and delete user names 
>
>    b.  create and delete file names and their associated passwords
>
>
>If you don't know the answers to the above, maybe you can email me or post
>to this list the name/email address of someone I could ask?
>
>At the moment, the ftp servers I've learned about are either "buggy" or are
>not industrial strength.
>
>TIA,
>
>Ray Sarna

NT's built-in FTP server uses the NT user accounts for FTP login and
password validation.  You can remotely administer the user accounts on an NT
machine from another NT machine, assuming you're logged in as a user name
which exists on both machines and has administrative rights on both
machines, and you've enabled the remote administration option (enabled by
default, I think).  This also assumes there is no firewall between you and
the remote NT machine that would block the network packets.

You can also remotely administer file priveledges on NTFS file partitions.
NTFS is the only way to specify per-user per-file access rights to files.
FTP has no choice but to respect those NTFS priveledges.

