From: Huebner.Fred <huebner@NIEHS.NIH.GOV>
Subject: RE: NT Clusters

Scott,

I saw a demo of the DEC NT clustering at a local NT user group meeting
recently.  Essentially, it makes several NT servers appear as a single
network entity (as you would expect) and currently is limited to pretty
much failover file service.  The product will do failover app service IF
the application has been written to take advantage of clustering, the
rep mentioned that MS backoffice was going to be supporting clustering
first.

The demo that was presented was of a Windows 95 machine playing a video
file from the cluster.  In the middle of the video, the machine serving
the data was detached from the shared SCSI bus (that's how they are
hooking the machines together physically).  Within several seconds the
other machine in the cluster picked up serving the file EXACTLY where
the first machine left off.  It was pretty impressive, but in no way as
polished as VAX Clustering.

I do not see how DEC is going to make any money on it though, the way
their development was described, it looked like DEC would be doing the
leading-edge work and selling that as an add-on to NT.  Several months
after the DEC release, MS would incorporate the clustering technology
into NT.  At that point, DEC would be forced to improve on clustering
and sell their improvements as an add-on product until the cycle comes
full circle and MS incorporates the DEC advances into the shipping
product.  To me, that is going to be a tough market when customers will
know they could sit six or nine months behind the leading edge for free.
 Just my opinion.

One thing that I saw that turned me off a bit to the whole thing was
that you needed to install a modified redirector on all of the cluster
clients, to allow them to utilize the technology, that could get to be
quite a task if you have a large client base.

Overall, I am glad to see clustering come to NT, and who better to bring
it to us that Digital.

BTW, I don't work for Digital and am in to way connected with the sale
or distribution of any of these products...etc.

Fred Huebner

