From: Tony Wilhelmsson <atotowi@ato.abb.se>
Subject: Re: NT & Raid

Len ! wrote:
> 
> What's the best recommended Raid technology I should implement in my server other than the tape backup and ups.
> 
> I have 6 - 4.3 GB drives and was planning on implementing
> Disk striping with Parity. How much disk should I allocate
> to achieve this and cover the disks?
> 
> How should I go about it?
> 
> Should I add more drives in the future, How will this effect
> what I have already set up ???
> 
> TIA
> 
> Len !Well I would say that RAID level 5 (striping with parity) is the way to go.

I would choose the a 5 disk configuration unless I was shure to get a new disk fast 
when a disk crashes. Because if another disk craches you would loose data.
How to configure the system in detail depends on how many/large partitions you want. 
Each disk can contain 4 primary patitions and a raid set is a primary parttion.
Striping ower few disk gives a large overhead due to parity data (3 disks 1/3 parity, 
4 disks 1/4, 5 disks 1/5 and so on). Striping over a large number of disks increases 
the risk of a single disk failure.

How to create a stripe set:
- Add disks in your system.
- In the Diskmanager select the disks and choose create stripe set with parity from 
the fault tolerance menu.
- Select the size of the stripe set and create it.
- Save the configuration and reboot the system.
- Use the diskmanager to format your stripe set.

If you add more disks in the future you will not be able to include these in an 
already created stripe set. So they will not effect what you already had set up.

My recomendation is to use a hardware raid system to create raid sets (mirrored, 
striped ...) this will give you a few but nice bennifits:
- Faster system since the calculation of parity data or double writing will be 
performed in hardware, not by your CPU.
- Greater flexibility when new disks are added. Since the raid sets is presented to NT 
as a single disk you can use volume sets to add diskspace to already defined 
partitions.
