From haigh@ntsolution.com Sat Jun 29 20:21:48 1996 CDT
Message-ID: <1996q2-0759.alphant@archive.invalid>
X-Archive-Original-Message-ID: <03223558502594@nckcn.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Jun 1996 19:59:20 -0400 (EDT)
From: Scott Haigh <haigh@ntsolution.com>
Subject: Re: NT & Raid
In-Reply-To: <01I6E9RH1BHU936YRU@CBE.AB.CA>

I would highly recommend that you pursue a hardware based raid solution 
as opposed to NT's built-in raid.  It may cost you more for the 
controller, however,  all the raid functions are performed by the 
controller with no overhead on the operating system.   I've used both 
DEC's and Compaq's RAID controllers and feel that DEC's is the superior 
product. Take a look at DEC's raid array 230.   

Pursue a raid 5 solution. RAID 5 maintains a good balance between 
performance and fault tolerance.   The more disks you include in the raid 
array, the better the ratio of disk space utilization to fault tolerance. For 
instance, if you use only 3 disks in a raid array,  you're only getting 
two-thirds of the disks's capacity since one disk is used for parity. 
However, if you use 10 disks in an array,  only one disk is still used 
for parity so you get a much better ratio of usable space.  This is 
especially true for high capacity drives like your 4.3 gb drives.   

