At 07:41 AM 6/28/1996 +0200, Tony Wilhelmsson wrote:
>Len ! wrote:
>> 
>> What's the best recommended Raid technology I should implement in my
server other than the tape backup and ups.
>> 
>> I have 6 - 4.3 GB drives and was planning on implementing
>> Disk striping with Parity. How much disk should I allocate
>> to achieve this and cover the disks?
>> 
>> How should I go about it?
>> 
>> Should I add more drives in the future, How will this effect
>> what I have already set up ???
>> 
>> TIA
>> 
>> Len !Well I would say that RAID level 5 (striping with parity) is the way
to go.
>
>I would choose the a 5 disk configuration unless I was shure to get a new
disk fast 
>when a disk crashes. Because if another disk craches you would loose data.
>

In a striped set, you need 9 drives to survive total failure of any one
device.  Parity error correction can only correct one missing bit per byte.
If you have 5 drives in a stripe set, you have two bits per drive on some of
the drives.  If one of those two-bit drives <g> fails, you lose all the data
in the stripe set because the parity error correction can't correct 2
missing bits.

5 drives per stripe set, with parity, gives you one bit error correction at
the disk sector level, so you can survive bad sectors without data loss.
Only 9 drive stripe sets can survive the failure of an entire drive,
assuming all the bits on the other 8 drives are perfect.

